  "note": "Tables 1 to 6 parsed from the PDF text layer by word positions (rotated drug headers rebuilt from characters, trend arrows stripped), count tables re-added by row and by column against their printed totals; every value of every extracted table then compared by eye with the rendered page images (full pages at scale 3 and zoomed crops), 2026-09-26. The archived PDF was re-downloaded and matched the census file byte for byte (MD5)."
 },
 "notes": "Half-yearly hospital antibiogram of AIIMS Bhopal, July to December 2022: 3093 isolates as printed. Rows are hospital-wide (spec all, set all): Tables 5 and 6 pool all specimens and all locations (OPD, IPD, ICU and Emergency), each cell giving percent susceptible over the number of isolates tested; drugs printed as -, NR, IR or R are left out. Counts: Tables 1 and 2 (by specimen, all locations) and Tables 3 and 4 (by location, all specimens) are two views of the same isolates, recorded as spec x all and all x setting, with each organism's Total from Table 1 or 2 as the all x all count; IPD is recorded as ward. The Emergency column and the Others specimen column have no key and are not recorded, nor are cells printed as \"-\", so the recorded location or specimen counts add up to less than each organism's total. Source inconsistency: Table 1 prints Klebsiella respiratory as \"20(0.64)\"; the organism total (463), the respiratory column total (308) and the printed 0.64% all imply 2. Recorded as printed (20). Source inconsistency: the Total column of the location table (Table 3 or 4) differs from the specimen table (Table 1 or 2, which agrees with Tables 5 and 6) for Burkholderia cepacia 13 (Table 1 or 2) vs 12 (Table 3 or 4); Enterobacter cloacae 71 (Table 1 or 2) vs 70 (Table 3 or 4); Escherichia coli 978 (Table 1 or 2) vs 996 (Table 3 or 4); Klebsiella Species 463 (Table 1 or 2) vs 448 (Table 3 or 4); Proteus Species 39 (Table 1 or 2) vs 36 (Table 3 or 4); Providencia Species 29 (Table 1 or 2) vs 27 (Table 3 or 4); Pseudomonas Species 360 (Table 1 or 2) vs 354 (Table 3 or 4); Typhoidal Salmonella 30 (Table 1 or 2) vs 31 (Table 3 or 4); Serratia Species 19 (Table 1 or 2) vs 20 (Table 3 or 4); Sphingomonas paucimobilis 17 (Table 1 or 2) vs 21 (Table 3 or 4); Stenotrophomonas maltophilia 33 (Table 1 or 2) vs 37 (Table 3 or 4). The specimen-table totals are recorded as the organism totals; location counts are as printed. 8 printed percentages cannot come from any whole number of the isolates printed as tested and are more than 1 point from the nearest possible value (or rest on a note that contradicts Table 6); they are kept as printed and marked as conflicts. 10 other printed percentages differ by up to 1 point from any whole number of the isolates printed as tested (the percentage and the tested count appear to use slightly different denominators); these are kept as printed. Archived copy used (the live site could not be reached): https://web.archive.org/web/20240716191317id_/https://aiimsbhopal.edu.in/AIIMSFiles/annexuredImage/Antibiogram_July-December_2022.pdf.",
 "issues": [
  "Second reader 2026-09-26: every cell re-read from the page images (scale 3, zoomed crops by column group) before comparing with the file: all 291 cells with their numbers tested (Tables 5 and 6, including the note values under each table), all 21 row n and all 163 counts (Tables 1 to 4). The seeded sample of 73 cells (sample_cells.py) is part of this. 0 transcription errors, nothing corrected: no column shift, and organism, n and specimen or location (OPD, IPD, ICU) correct on every row and count.",
  "Second reader: Klebsiella respiratory count confirmed as printed \"20(0.64)\" in Table 1: the row adds to 481 against its printed total 463, and 0.64% of the 308 respiratory total, like the column sum, gives 2. A source typo, kept as printed (20) as the notes say. The 11 organism totals that differ between Table 1 and Table 3 (listed in notes) are printed that way; otherwise Tables 1 to 4 add up by row and by column.",
  "Second reader: the 19 cells the validator does not keep are printed as recorded: 9 intrinsic (B. cepacia gentamicin and amikacin 11.1, B. pseudomallei gentamicin 23.5, Morganella minocycline 8.7, Proteus and Providencia minocycline 5.3, Pseudomonas nitrofurantoin 25, Typhoidal Salmonella cefuroxime 0, E. faecalis co-trimoxazole 100) and 10 cautions (Serratia cefepime 55.6, ertapenem 80, meropenem 61.1, ciprofloxacin 58.8, levofloxacin 76.9, imipenem 66.7, cefoperazone-sulbactam 38.9; E. faecalis ampicillin 94.7 of 1, erythromycin 7.3 of 40, daptomycin 38.2 of 8). In the Serratia row every printed percentage fits a denominator one below the printed number tested (cefepime 55.6% = 10 of 18, 19 printed).",
  "Second reader, source error found (row note added, no value changed): Table 6 prints the E. faecalis numbers tested one column out of line with its percentages. 9 of its percentages do not fit the number printed under them, but each is exact against the number printed one column to the left: levofloxacin 15% = 6 of 40, ampicillin 94.7% = 18 of 19, penicillin 56.1% = 23 of 41, vancomycin 79.5% = 31 of 39, erythromycin 7.3% = 3 of 41, nitrofurantoin 45% = 9 of 20, tigecycline 84.8% = 28 of 33, daptomycin 38.2% = 13 of 34, tetracycline 20.5% = 8 of 39. The percentages sit under the right drugs (vancomycin 79.5% matches the report's 20.5% vancomycin-resistant E. faecalis); the row's nt values are unreliable. For the lead: decide whether to mark these cells as conflicts or drop this row's nt.",
  "Lead 2026-09-26: E. faecalis (Table 6) numbers tested are misaligned by one column in the source: the row keeps its printed percentages, each marked as a conflict (caution), and the numbers tested are left out."
 ],
